{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 New York Yankees were a team built on depth, resilience, and a relentless barrage of home runs. From the very first week of the season, young players like Gleyber Torres and Clint Frazier announced themselves with massive swings, while veterans like Brett Gardner found new life. The Yankees lost two of their first three games but quickly righted the ship, emerging from April as legitimate AL East contenders with a 16-10 record carried by a lineup that simply refused to stop mashing.\n\nMay and June represented the Yankees' full ascent into dominance. A historic 20-7 May — fueled by unexpected heroes like Gio Urshela at third base and Gary Sánchez catching fire — pushed New York to the top of the division. The bullpen stumbled at moments, surrendering key blasts to Vladimir Guerrero Jr. and Yordan Alvarez, but the offense consistently bailed the team out. DJ LeMahieu, the quiet free-agent addition, proved to be the perfect glue, delivering clutch hit after clutch hit. Even with a slightly more modest 17-9 June, the Yankees had established themselves as one of baseball's best teams.\n\nJuly brought turbulence and heroics in equal measure. Aroldis Chapman, the fire-breathing closer, was torched by Travis d'Arnaud for a brutal three-run homer that wiped out a lead, yet Aaron Judge roared back the next day with a go-ahead shot of his own. Aaron Hicks delivered perhaps the most electrifying regular-season moment of the year — a walk-off two-run blast against Minnesota on July 23 that swung 72.7% of the win probability in one swing. The Yankees went 14-11 in July, but felt like a team primed for a monster finish. And a monster finish they delivered: a 21-9 August, the best month of the season, confirming this was a 100-win club in the making.\n\nThe postseason told a different story. The Yankees dispatched Minnesota in the ALDS but ran into the Houston Astros' buzzsaw in the ALCS. J.A. Happ was rocked by Carlos Correa, Chad Green surrendered a crushing three-run homer to Yuli Gurriel, and most devastatingly, Chapman — the man trusted to slam the door — gave up a two-run walk-off shot to Jose Altuve in Game 6 that ended New York's season. After 103 wins and 306 home runs, it all came down to one swing by Altuve, and the Yankees were left to wonder what might have been.",
